#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# normal_distribution

#% text
# `normal_distribution(mu, sigma)` represents the normal (Gaussian) distribution with mean `mu` and standard deviation `sigma`.

#% text
# `normal_distribution()` is the standard normal distribution, `normal_distribution(0, 1)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The density is exp(-(x - mu)^2/(2 sigma^2))/(sigma sqrt(2 pi)).

#% item
# The parameters may be numbers or symbols; a numeric `sigma` must be positive.

#% item
# The distribution is a value for `pdf`, `cdf`, `survival`, `quantile`, `mean`, `variance`, `std_dev`, `median`, `random_variate` and the other functions of distributions.

#% item
# With machine reals the results are machine reals; with exact numbers they are exact, in terms of `erfc` and `inverse_erfc`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The density of the standard normal distribution:

#% cell
pdf(normal_distribution(), x)
#% output
#= 2^(1/2)*exp((-1/2)*x^2)/(2*pi^(1/2))

#% text
# The probability of a value below 1.96:

#% cell
cdf(normal_distribution(), 1.96)
#% output
#= 0.9750021048517795

#% text
# The mean and variance:

#% cell
[mean(normal_distribution(3, 2)), variance(normal_distribution(3, 2))]
#% output
#= [3, 4]

#% text
# The density as a curve:

#% cell
plot(pdf(normal_distribution(), x), [x, -4, 4])
#% output
#= image("normal_distribution.files/normal_distribution-4.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Symbolic parameters:

#% cell
pdf(normal_distribution(mu, sigma), x)
#% output
#= 2^(1/2)*exp(-(x - mu)^2/(2*sigma^2))/(2*pi^(1/2)*sigma)

#% cell
cdf(normal_distribution(), x)
#% output
#= (1/2)*erfc((-1/2)*x*2^(1/2))

#% text
# The 97.5% quantile, numerically and exactly:

#% cell
quantile(normal_distribution(), 0.975)
#% output
#= 1.9599639845400538

#% cell
quantile(normal_distribution(), 975/1000)
#% output
#= -inverse_erfc(39/20)*2^(1/2)

#% text
# Skewness and kurtosis:

#% cell
[skewness(normal_distribution(2, 3)), kurtosis(normal_distribution(2, 3))]
#% output
#= [0, 3]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The share of a population within two standard deviations of the mean, for test scores with mean 100 and standard deviation 15:

#% cell
n(cdf(normal_distribution(100, 15), 130) - cdf(normal_distribution(100, 15), 70))
#% output
#= 0.9544997361036416

#% text
# Estimate the parameters from data:

#% cell
seed(3); estimated_distribution(random_variate(normal_distribution(5, 2), 1000), normal_distribution(m, s))
#% output
#= normal_distribution(5.041948397014239, 1.9992946087893353)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The median equals the mean `mu`:

#% cell
median(normal_distribution(mu, sigma))
#% output
#= mu

#% text
# `survival` stays accurate far in the tail, where 1 - `cdf` rounds to zero:

#% cell
[survival(normal_distribution(), 10.0), 1 - cdf(normal_distribution(), 10.0)]
#% output
#= [7.619853024160523e-24, 0.0]

#% text
# `quantile` inverts `cdf`:

#% cell
cdf(normal_distribution(), quantile(normal_distribution(), 0.9))
#% output
#= 0.9

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The standard deviation must be positive:

#% cell
normal_distribution(0, 0)
#% output
#! failure(argument, normal_distribution, "the standard deviation sigma must be positive")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `pdf`, `cdf`, `quantile`, `random_variate`, `estimated_distribution`, `log_normal_distribution`, `student_t_distribution`
